<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">PIDE</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1933">1933</a>–<a href="/years/1974">1974</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as PVDE, International and State Defence Police</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">PIDE was the political police of Portugal's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo</a>. Founded in 1933 as the PVDE, it was renamed PIDE in 1945 and DGS in 1969. It censored, watched and arrested opponents of the regime, used torture and imprisonment without trial, and ran the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarrafal-camp">Tarrafal camp</a>, and it also fought nationalist movements in the African colonies. It was abolished after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnation-revolution">Carnation Revolution</a> of 1974.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The force grew out of a long line of police reorganisations under the First Republic and the Ditadura Nacional. With Salazar as prime minister, the Political and Social Surveillance Section became an autonomous police in January 1933 and in August merged with the Portuguese International Police as the PVDE, by Decree-Law 22992. Its founder and head, Captain Agostinho Lourenço, kept close ties with MI6 and became president of Interpol in 1956. In 1936 the Tarrafal prison camp was opened under its control in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-verde">Cape Verde</a>; 32 people died there over the years of the regime.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> and an anarchist attempt on Salazar's life in 1937 the PVDE turned chiefly against the underground Portuguese Communist Party, with Italian and German advisers helping it to adopt a model like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gestapo">Gestapo</a>'s. During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> neutral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> became a centre of espionage, but the PVDE stayed neutral toward foreign spies as long as they kept out of Portuguese politics. Renamed PIDE in 1945, it was presented by the regime as following the Scotland Yard model and was given full powers to investigate, detain and arrest.</p><p>Many authors count it among the most effective secret services in history. It placed agents in almost every underground movement, including the independence movements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, and rewarded informers, called bufos. It vetted candidates for public employment and could keep activists in prison after their sentences ended; the law student Aurora Rodrigues was kept awake for weeks and subjected to simulated drowning. In the Colonial War it formed the paramilitary Flechas. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcelo-caetano">Marcelo Caetano</a> its violence lessened, and with it its effectiveness.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>On 25 April 1974 agents firing from the headquarters on António Maria Cardoso Street in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> killed four demonstrators, and one agent was killed while fleeing: the only deaths of the revolution. Most records were destroyed, and many agents escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, though the director-general Silva Pais was among those captured; 89 broke out of Alcoentre penitentiary in June 1975. In the colonies the service lived on as the Military Information Police until 1975. Only the killers of Humberto Delgado were tried, in absentia, and none served time. Its surviving papers have been in the Torre do Tombo National Archive since 1990, and Portugal set up a new intelligence system, SIRP, only in 1984.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “PIDE” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The regime banned parties except the National Union, censored the press and used the <b>PVDE</b>, later PIDE, secret police and the Tarrafal prison camp against opponents.</blockquote><a href="/state/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo (Portugal)</a></li></ul></section>
